\begin{proof}[\wtag\ Pointer]
This is source~14's Theorem~\ref{smc:tm:thm:certificate}, for the same polynomial. Source~15's proof repeats source~14's (the square $(E_t-1)^2$ and natural selectors force one-hot selection, every inactive product forces its offset to zero, the active offset is $N-1$, $N/p-1$ or $(N-r)/p$, the terminal square forces a first halt, and $h=0$ leaves only the constant halt square); it is not reprinted.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}[\wtag\ Pointer]
This is source~16's Lemmas~\ref{smc:su:lem:invariant} and~\ref{smc:su:lem:walker}, proved there by the same argument (the cases $d>S$ and $d\le S$, with all new support in $[d-S,S]$, then finitely many normalized shapes); not reprinted.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}[\wtag\ Pointer]
This is source~16's Proposition~\ref{smc:su:prop:orbits}, with Lemmas~\ref{smc:su:lem:outside} and~\ref{smc:su:lem:first} for the first entry into the core of diameter $4S$ and the finite graph of core returns; not reprinted.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}[\wtag\ Pointer]
This is Proposition~\ref{smc:sl:prop:bounded} of Part~IV (source~13), proved there by the same finite-shape argument: simulate until the diameter escapes or a normalized shape repeats, a repeat giving the translating tail. Source~14's proof is not reprinted.
\end{proof}
